Environment‑Defined Mathematics (EDM) is a novel, origin‑layer operational substrate and algebraic framework governing system lifecycle, routing, constraints, remediation, 
provenance, termination, and audit through environment‑defined operators 𝐸.

In EDM, the execution environment is the foundational mathematical actor: no computational state transition, lifecycle event, or operator exists unless explicitly instantiated, 
bounded, and governed by the environment operator 𝐸.

This establishes EDM as a new mathematical class, not reducible to probability theory, temporal logic, control theory, category theory, type theory, cryptography, or any 
existing discipline.

By defining the environment as the primary operator, EDM becomes an enforceable, functional control architecture suitable for licensing, regulatory classification, and 
cross‑domain compliance.

Operators instantiated by 𝐸 are inseparable from the SSPS substrate, and any system performing these functions automatically invokes SSPS licensing obligations.

